{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong positive level bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (often by ~+2 to +8 EUR), especially after mid-August; apply a downward offset that increases with forecast horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: model extrapolates recent strength and misses regime shifts; when the last 5–10 days show weakening/volatility, damp the forecast slope (reduce day-to-day drift toward the recent trend).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following local highs (e.g., ~71–73), the model projects continued gains, but realized prices mean-revert and can sell off sharply; cap upside continuation and force partial mean reversion toward the 20–30d average after a spike.",
    "Horizon-specific error: near-term (days 1–5) errors are smaller, but medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors blow out and stay too bullish; progressively shrink deviations from the current spot/short-term mean as horizon extends (e.g., stronger reversion weight beyond day ~7–10).",
    "Volatility underestimation on downside: realized paths show faster, deeper drawdowns than forecast; increase downside responsiveness by allowing larger negative moves when recent returns turn negative (asymmetric adjustment: stronger correction when momentum flips down)."
  ]
}
